  {"question": "In arXiv 2502.18576, how did youth use domain expertise and generational trends to audit AI systems?", "answer": "Youth leveraged domain expertise, such as knowledge about sharks or crocheting, and generational trends like 'brain rot' to test AI limitations and identify problematic outputs.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "A2 had deep knowledge of crocheting, so she took a factchecking approach in using genAI to create images of crocheted garments. On her auditing log, A2 reported that the generated image did not have 'recognizable stitches' and that it looked 'glitchy,' pointing out that the images generated were factually incorrect, given the stitches' appearances in the generated images (Figure 1c)."}, {"page": 8, "quote": "For example, both B2 and B6 who sat at separate tables (i.e., they did not directly converse with one another in the session) input 'brain rot' into the genAI. B2 specifically noted that she chose to try out this input because it was 'a term only people [her] age use.'"}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2502.18576, what processes did youth suggest for handling AI audit reports, and who did they think should be responsible?", "answer": "Youth suggested that AI creators or industry leaders should handle audit reports, proposing grouping issues and ranking reports by harm, but also expressed skepticism about company motives.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "Reports should go back to whoever developed the AI to try and help them reduce bias, ' a 'higher-up CEO,' or 'people researching AI.' Youth considered ways that the companies could prioritize surfaced issues, including processes like 'put [the reports] into groups with similar issues [so that] the problems can be conquered quicker,' or 'rank reports based on how users ranked how harmful they are. Then have a way to determine how accurate that harm is.' However, some participants expressed skepticism about companies handling things satisfactorily, one noting that they 'won't [handle the reports, because] they just want money.'"}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2502.18576, what is the definition of 'critical AI literacy' and how is it used in the workshop?", "answer": "Critical AI literacy is understanding how AI functions and its societal and ethical impacts; it was fostered through interventions and guided discussions in the workshop.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "critical AI literacy, i.e., understanding not only how AI functions but the ability to consider societal and ethical impacts [62, 66, 68], is core to supporting youth in thoughtfully navigating everyday AI systems."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2502.18576, what are the six main topics youth used as inputs to audit AI?", "answer": "The six main topics are: Controversial Topics, Fact Checks, Generational Trends, Open-ended Exploration, Identity-related Topics, and Creative Scenarios.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "Our thematic analysis of youths' auditing logs and auditing tool reports revealed six main topics that youth intentionally used and hypothesized about. Controversial Topics ... Fact Checks ... Generational Trends ... Open-ended Exploration ... Identity-related Topics ... Creative Scenarios ..."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2502.18576, which AI systems did youth audit during the workshop, and how was 'breaking' defined?", "answer": "Youth audited DeepAI (text-to-image genAI), Google Images (curative AI), and Google Search Bar (recommendation AI); 'breaking' meant making the AI output something incorrect, disagreeable, or potentially harmful.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "participants used individual laptops and were tasked to 'break' three types of AI: DeepAI (text-to-image genAI), Google Images (curative AI), and Google Search Bar (recommendation AI). We defined 'breaking' the AI as making the AI output something that the youth thought was incorrect, they disagreed with, or could potentially have harmful effects on people."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2502.18576, which participant had expertise in sharks and how did she use it during AI auditing?", "answer": "Participant B1 had strong domain expertise in sharks and used it to check facts and representations of sharks in AI outputs.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "Another teen, B1, had strong domain expertise in sharks. Many of her attempts to audit the AI included checking facts on information and representations of certain types of sharks."}]}
]}
